Q: TCP vs UDP?

| Feature            | TCP                         | UDP                        |
| ------------------ | --------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Connection         | Connection-oriented         | Connectionless             |
| Reliability        | Reliable (ack/retransmit)   | Best-effort                |
| Ordering           | Ordered                     | Not guaranteed             |
| Speed / overhead   | More overhead               | Lower latency/overhead     |
| Use cases          | HTTP, HTTPS, SSH, DB, Kafka | DNS, video, voice, gaming, QUIC/HTTP/3 |

One-liner:
TCP = reliable phone call; UDP = sending postcards (fast, may lose some).

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ Do we code to use TCP or UDP?

Usually **NO** — not in normal Spring Boot API code.

You write:
@GetMapping("/users")
public List<User> getUsers() { ... }

Under the hood:
HTTP → TCP → IP

Tomcat/Netty already uses **TCP**. You didn’t choose it in the controller.

| What you use              | Transport underneath |
| ------------------------- | -------------------- |
| Spring REST / Postman HTTP| TCP                  |
| HTTPS                     | TCP (+ TLS)          |
| JDBC to MySQL/Postgres    | TCP                  |
| Kafka client              | TCP                  |
| Redis client              | TCP                  |
| RabbitMQ AMQP             | TCP                  |

When would you write TCP/UDP code yourself?
- Rare low-level networking (`Socket`, `DatagramSocket` in Java)
- Custom game/IoT/streaming protocols
- Most business apps: frameworks choose for you

